    "### Q2(a) · Tanh vs ReLU/Sigmoid in the Generator &nbsp; \n",
    "\n",
    "Why does the Generator use **Tanh** as its final activation instead of ReLU or Sigmoid?\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "94a382da",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "****Tanh** outputs values between **-1 and 1**, which matches the normalized pixel range commonly used in GANs. This helps the generator produce more balanced images and makes training more stable. In contrast, **ReLU** can output unbounded positive values, and **Sigmoid** restricts outputs to **0–1**, which is often less suitable for image generation.\n",
    "**\n",
    "\n",
    "> YOUR ANSWER HERE\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "38f2bd37",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Q2(b) · LeakyReLU in the Discriminator &nbsp;\n",
    "\n",
    "Why does the Discriminator use **LeakyReLU** instead of standard ReLU?\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "3ae3679e",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**LeakyReLU allows a small, non-zero gradient for negative inputs, so neurons continue learning even when the input is negative. This helps prevent the \"dying ReLU\" problem, where some neurons stop updating completely. As a result, the discriminator learns more effectively and training becomes more stable.**\n",
    "\n",
    "> YOUR ANSWER HERE\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "3f3a8b8e",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Q2(c) · DCGAN — Benefits of Convolutions &nbsp; \n",
    "\n",
    "DCGAN replaces fully-connected layers with convolutional layers.  \n",
    "Name **two specific benefits** of this change.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "1db7055a",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**Your Answer:**\n",
    "\n",
    "> 1. Better feature extraction: Convolutional layers capture local patterns such as edges, textures, and shapes, which are important for generating and recognizing images.\n",
    "> 2. Fewer parameters: Convolutional layers use weight sharing, significantly reducing the number of parameters. This makes the model more efficient and helps reduce overfitting.\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "ee3da688",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "### Q2(d) · No BatchNorm in Discriminator &nbsp; \n",
    "\n",
    "Why is **BatchNorm NOT recommended** inside the Discriminator,  \n",
    "even though it stabilises the Generator?\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "id": "4834425c",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "**Your Answer:**\n",
    "\n",
    "> BatchNorm uses statistics from the entire batch, which can mix information between real and fake samples. This may make it easier for the discriminator to distinguish samples based on batch statistics rather than actual image features. As a result, the discriminator can become less reliable and GAN training may become unstable.\n",
    "\n",
    "---\n"
   ]
  },
